    {
      "section_id": "SEC045",
      "title": "Feed Them Afterward",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Jesus himself, after a miraculous spiritual act, gave instruction to feed the resurrected. When he resurrected the man who had been fasting for four days, the first instruction was to give him food to eat. He also resurrected the daughter of Jairus, and gave her food to eat. Physical needs must be addressed even after a miraculous spiritual act.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA173",
            "EX029",
            "SRC004743",
            "SRC004744",
            "SRC004748",
            "SRC004751",
            "SRC004752"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC004743",
            "SRC004744",
            "SRC004748",
            "SRC004751",
            "SRC004752"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA173"
          ],
          "example_refs": [
            "EX029"
          ],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000009",
          "provider_handle": "p9"
        },
        {
          "text": "This calls for discernment in pastoral practice, particularly concerning fasting. Sometimes some people are already very, very sick, and yet they are put on a fast of seven days. Then one wonders why the prayer didn't work, when in fact the fast is about to kill them. Prolonged fasting imposed on people who are already weak or sick can be dangerous rather than spiritually effective; it requires discernment.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA174",
            "SRC004756",
            "SRC004757",
            "SRC004758",
            "SRC004761"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC004756",
            "SRC004757",
            "SRC004758",
            "SRC004761"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA174"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000010",
          "provider_handle": "p10"
        }
      ],
      "idea_refs": [
        "IDEA173",
        "IDEA174"
      ],
      "source_refs": [
        "SRC004743",
        "SRC004744",
        "SRC004748",
        "SRC004751",
        "SRC004752",
        "SRC004756",
        "SRC004757",
        "SRC004758",
        "SRC004761"
      ]
    },
    {
      "section_id": "SEC046",
      "title": "Bringing Every Thought Captive",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "The weapon of my warfare is not natural, but it is mighty through God to pull down strongholds.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA175",
            "SRC004767",
            "SRC004768"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC004767",
            "SRC004768"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA175"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000011",
          "provider_handle": "p11"
        },
        {
          "text": "We cannot wage war against a world cut off from reasonings, from the thoughts that set themselves up against the knowledge of God. The real combat is not physical; it takes place in the realm of reasonings and knowledge, in whatever opposes the knowledge of God.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA176",
            "SRC004790",
            "SRC004795",
            "SRC004799",
            "SRC004804"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC004790",
            "SRC004795",
            "SRC004799",
            "SRC004804"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA176"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000012",
          "provider_handle": "p12"
        },
        {
          "text": "We are called to take every thought captive — we bring every thought captive. This is repeated insistently, again and again, as a discipline to be practiced against the enemy's infiltration of the mind.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA177",
            "SRC004813",
            "SRC004816",
            "SRC004842",
            "SRC004843"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC004813",
            "SRC004816",
            "SRC004842",
            "SRC004843",
            "SRC004820",
            "SRC004830",
            "SRC004840"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA177"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [
            "UNC022"
          ],
          "paragraph_id": "P000013",
          "provider_handle": "p13"
        },
        {
          "text": "Because if the enemy succeeds in penetrating this barrier, praying twenty-four hours a day, without stopping, for three hundred and sixty-five days will not undo what has already been breached. The barrier of the mind must be guarded before the breach, not repaired afterward by sheer length of prayer.",
          "kind": "substantive",
          "evidence_handles": [
            "IDEA178",
            "SRC004842",
            "SRC004843",
            "SRC004844",
            "SRC004845"
          ],
          "source_refs": [
            "SRC004842",
            "SRC004843",
            "SRC004844",
            "SRC004845"
          ],
          "idea_refs": [
            "IDEA178"
          ],
          "example_refs": [],
          "reference_refs": [],
          "uncertainty_refs": [],
          "paragraph_id": "P000014",
          "provider_handle": "p14"
        }
      ],
